---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import CaseBuilder from '../../components/CaseBuilder.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="SQL CASE Statement Builder" description="Build a SQL CASE WHEN expression visually with multiple conditions.">
  <CaseBuilder client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why CASE is SQL's conditional logic</h2>
  <p>CASE WHEN lets you compute conditional values directly inside a query — turning a numeric score into a letter grade, or a status code into a readable label — without needing application code to post-process every row.</p>

  <h2>How to use this builder</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Add one condition-result pair per WHEN clause you need.</li>
    <li>Set an optional ELSE default for unmatched rows.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated CASE expression into your SELECT statement.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <pre><code>CASE
  WHEN score >= 90 THEN 'A'
  WHEN score >= 80 THEN 'B'
  ELSE 'C'
END AS grade</code></pre>

  <h2>Common CASE Statement Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting WHEN conditions are evaluated in order</strong> — the first matching condition wins, so ordering from most to least specific matters.</li>
    <li><strong>Omitting ELSE</strong> when you actually need a default — unmatched rows return NULL instead of a sensible fallback.</li>
    <li><strong>Comparing against NULL with <code>=</code></strong> inside a WHEN clause, which never evaluates true — use <code>IS NULL</code> instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This CASE Builder</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Converting numeric scores into categorical labels in a report</li>
    <li>Creating a readable status column from internal status codes</li>
    <li>Conditional aggregation, like counting rows that meet different criteria in one query</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>SQL CASE Statement FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Can CASE be used outside SELECT?</strong> Yes — it's also valid in WHERE, ORDER BY, and other clauses wherever an expression is allowed.</p>
</ToolLayout>
